---
import Breadcrumbs from './Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ToolCallFlow from './ToolCallFlow.astro';
import NextSteps from './NextSteps.astro';
---

<!-- Breadcrumbs -->
<Breadcrumbs items={[
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Agentic Use', href: '/agentic-use' },
  { label: 'Tool Calling' },
]} />

<!-- Hero -->
<header class="mb-12">
  <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
    <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal" />
    <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Agentic Use</span>
  </span>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-heading font-semibold text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    Tool Calling
  </h1>
  <p class="text-base text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose mb-4">
    Giving the agent hands.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-prose">
    A language model alone is like a brilliant mind trapped behind glass — it can reason, create,
    and analyze, but it cannot <em>act</em> on the world. Tool calling gives agents the ability
    to reach beyond their training data: searching the web, running code, querying databases,
    and interacting with APIs in real time.
  </p>
</header>

<!-- Interactive visualization -->
<div class="mb-12">
  <ToolCallFlow />
</div>

<!-- What is tool calling? -->
<section class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    What is Tool Calling?
  </h2>
  <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed space-y-3">
    <p>
      Tool calling (also known as <em>function calling</em>) is the mechanism by which a language
      model decides to invoke an external tool — and generates the structured arguments needed
      to make that call.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think of it as the model saying: <em>"I don't have this information, but I know exactly
      which tool can get it — and I know what arguments to pass."</em> The model outputs a
      structured JSON object specifying the tool name and its parameters, which the runtime
      then executes.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Why it matters -->
<section class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    Why It Matters
  </h2>
  <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed space-y-3">
    <p>
      Without tool calling, a language model is limited to what it learned during training.
      Its knowledge is frozen at the training cutoff, it cannot access live data, and it
      cannot perform actions in the real world.
    </p>
    <p>
      With tool calling, agents become genuinely useful assistants that can:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-none space-y-2 ml-4">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <span class="text-teal mt-1">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span>Search the web for current information</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <span class="text-teal mt-1">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span>Run code and compute precise results</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <span class="text-teal mt-1">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span>Query databases and external APIs</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <span class="text-teal mt-1">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span>Orchestrate multi-step workflows autonomously</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- How it works -->
<section class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    How It Works
  </h2>
  <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed space-y-3">
    <p>
      The tool calling flow follows a structured cycle that repeats until the agent can
      provide a complete answer:
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Step breakdown -->
  <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm bg-teal/10 text-teal font-mono font-medium">
        1
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-1">User Prompt</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
          You send a request that requires information or action beyond the model's training data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm bg-teal/10 text-teal font-mono font-medium">
        2
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-1">Agent Thinks</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
          The model analyzes the request and determines that a tool is needed to provide an accurate answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm bg-teal/10 text-teal font-mono font-medium">
        3
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-1">Tool Call</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
          The model generates structured JSON specifying which tool to invoke and with what parameters.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm bg-teal/10 text-teal font-mono font-medium">
        4
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-1">Tool Result</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
          The runtime executes the tool (search, computation, API call) and feeds the result back into the model.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 5 -->
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm bg-teal/10 text-teal font-mono font-medium">
        5
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-near-black mb-1">Agent Response</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
          Now equipped with real-world data, the model generates a grounded, helpful response.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
    <p>
      The interactive visualization above lets you step through this flow with different tool types.
      Try switching between <strong>Web Search</strong>, <strong>Calculator</strong>, and
      <strong>Weather Lookup</strong> to see how the same flow adapts to different tools.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Tool definitions -->
<section class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    Defining Tools
  </h2>
  <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed space-y-3">
    <p>
      Before an agent can use a tool, the tool must be defined with a schema that tells the model
      what it does and what arguments it accepts. This is typically a JSON Schema describing:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-none space-y-1.5 ml-4">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">
          <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span><code class="text-xs font-mono bg-gray-50 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">name</code> — The tool identifier</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">
          <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span><code class="text-xs font-mono bg-gray-50 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">description</code> — What the tool does</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
        <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">
          <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span><code class="text-xs font-mono bg-gray-50 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">parameters</code> — Required inputs with types</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The model uses these definitions to decide <em>when</em> to call a tool and <em>how</em>
      to construct valid arguments. Good tool descriptions are critical — they guide the model
      toward correct usage.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Multi-turn flows -->
<section class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-heading font-medium text-near-black tracking-tight mb-3">
    Multi-Turn Flows
  </h2>
  <div class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed space-y-3">
    <p>
      Complex tasks often require multiple tool calls. An agent might search the web, then use
      a calculator to process the results, then look up additional context — all within a
      single conversation turn.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is where tool calling truly shines: agents can orchestrate chains of tool invocations,
      each building on the results of the previous, to solve problems that no single tool could
      handle alone.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<NextSteps
  accentColor="var(--color-teal)"
  items={[
    { label: 'Skills', href: '/skills', description: 'Reusable knowledge packages for domain expertise' },
  ]}
/>